APS-019 — Policy Conformance

Document ID: APS-019
Title: Policy Conformance
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Policy Conformance Model (CPCM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Policy Conformance Model establishes the principles, requirements, capability levels, verification mechanisms, and certification criteria by which implementations demonstrate conformance to the Atlas Policy Specification (APS).

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Conformance architecture
Conformance requirements
Capability profiles
Verification methods
Certification criteria
Conformance declarations
Interoperability verification
Continuous conformance

This specification applies to every implementation claiming compliance with APS.

3. Canonical Definition

Policy Conformance is the demonstrable satisfaction of the normative requirements of the Atlas Policy Specification by an implementation, supported through objective verification and evidence.

Conformance measures implementation.

It SHALL NOT redefine the specification.

4. Objectives

The Policy Conformance Model SHALL:

Enable objective verification.
Promote interoperability.
Support certification.
Preserve consistency.
Enable repeatable testing.
Support federation.
Preserve trust.
Encourage continuous improvement.
5. Conformance Principles

Every conformance assessment SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Normative Verification

Only normative ("SHALL") requirements SHALL determine conformance.

Evidence-Based

Conformance SHALL be supported by objective evidence.

Repeatability

Equivalent implementations SHALL produce equivalent conformance results.

Independence

Conformance assessment SHOULD be performed independently of implementation authorship.

Transparency

Assessment methods and results SHALL be documented.

Traceability

Every conformance claim SHALL reference the specific normative requirements it satisfies.

6. Conformance Targets

Atlas recognizes the following conformance targets.

Target	Description
Policy Engine	Policy evaluation implementation
Governance Platform	Policy governance implementation
Runtime	Policy enforcement implementation
Repository	Policy storage and distribution
Federation Node	Federated governance participant
Organization	Organizational governance implementation
Tooling	Supporting authoring and management tools

Each target SHALL declare its supported capability profile.

7. Capability Profiles

Atlas defines canonical capability profiles.

Profile	Description
Core	Mandatory APS capabilities
Standard	Core + governance capabilities
Advanced	Standard + federation + automation
Enterprise	Advanced + large-scale governance
Constitutional	Full APS implementation including federation, certification, and assurance

Profiles SHALL be cumulative.

8. Conformance Requirements

Every implementation SHALL declare:

Supported APS version.
Supported capability profile.
Supported policy families.
Supported lifecycle states.
Supported evaluation semantics.
Supported governance features.
Known limitations.

Declarations SHALL be publicly accessible.

9. Verification Methods

Atlas recognizes the following verification methods.

Method	Description
Document Review	Specification review
Functional Testing	Behavioral verification
Interoperability Testing	Cross-platform validation
Audit	Independent assessment
Simulation	Controlled evaluation scenarios
Certification Assessment	Formal verification

Multiple verification methods MAY be combined.

10. Conformance Test Suite

Every implementation SHALL be evaluated against:

Structural conformance tests
Semantic conformance tests
Evaluation tests
Governance tests
Security tests
Interoperability tests
Performance verification
Regression tests

Test suites SHALL be versioned.

11. Interoperability

Conforming implementations SHALL preserve:

Canonical semantics
Policy identity
Version compatibility
Governance metadata
Evaluation behavior
Audit traceability

Interoperability SHALL be independently testable.

12. Conformance Declaration

Every implementation SHALL publish a Conformance Declaration containing:

Implementation name
Vendor or organization
Supported APS version
Capability profile
Verified modules
Certification status
Assessment date
Evidence references

Declarations SHALL themselves be governed artifacts.

13. Certification

Atlas recognizes the following certification states.

State	Description
Self-Assessed	Declared by implementer
Independently Verified	Reviewed by third party
Certified	Approved by recognized authority
Conditionally Certified	Temporary limitations
Suspended	Certification temporarily inactive
Revoked	Certification withdrawn

Certification SHALL reference supporting evidence.

14. Continuous Conformance

Conformance SHALL be reassessed following:

Major APS revisions
Significant implementation changes
Security incidents
Governance changes
Certification renewal
Regulatory changes

Continuous conformance SHALL preserve certification integrity.

15. Conformance Governance

Every conformance assessment SHALL define:

Assessment authority
Certification authority
Applicable APS version
Capability profile
Evidence requirements
Renewal schedule

Governance SHALL remain independently auditable.

16. Quality Requirements

Every conformance assessment SHALL satisfy:

Accuracy
Objectivity
Repeatability
Traceability
Explainability
Auditability
Reproducibility
Transparency

Quality SHALL be continuously monitored.

17. Conformance Lifecycle

Every conformance assessment SHALL support:

Requested
     │
Prepared
     │
Assessed
     │
Verified
     │
Certified
     │
Monitored
     │
Renewed
     │
Expired

Historical assessments SHALL remain permanently accessible.

18. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

AI-assisted conformance assessment.
Machine-verifiable certification.
Continuous interoperability testing.
Federated certification authorities.
Automated compliance scoring.
Real-time conformance monitoring.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical conformance semantics.

